Package: mos-kernel-x64
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: kernel
Priority: optional
Description: mos kernel -- x64 (linux 6.12 LTS, configured for the verity boot)
 The Linux kernel the x64 board boots, built from the tag pinned in
 boards/x64/bsp/kernel/versions.env against the configuration recorded in
 boards/x64/bsp/kernel/config/. It ships /boot/vmlinuz-<release>, the
 /boot/config-<release> it was built from, and /usr/lib/modules/<release>/.
 .
 It replaces Debian's linux-image-amd64, and the reason is the boot path rather
 than size. Debian's generic kernel has no CONFIG_DM_INIT, so the
 dm-mod.create= verity table on the kernel command line was ignored on this
 board and an initramfs had to re-implement it -- a second reader of a contract
 cx3576's kernel reads directly. This kernel carries the device mapper, the
 dm-verity target and squashfs built in, so the root is assembled before the
 mount and the board ships no initrd at all.
 .
 It is NOT a generic distribution kernel and does not try to be. It carries
 virtio, AHCI, NVMe and USB mass storage for the root device and the virtio,
 Intel and Realtek network drivers, and nothing else: 4587 recorded config
 lines against Debian's 6673, and no wireless, sound, DRM or media stack, for a
 board whose declared role is the QEMU and CI baseline.
 .
 No Depends: the payload is a kernel image and its modules, and nothing in it
 links a shared library. It is not the boot chain either -- a UEFI machine's
 firmware and GRUB provide that -- so nothing is enabled and nothing runs at
 install time.
